# Proposal comparison page

The comparison page puts whole-page proposals that are already built side by side at their real size, and the user picks one on the page. The page only shows and collects: it never ranks, recommends, or chooses, and it writes nothing into the project. What goes on it, and what happens after the choice, are yours and the user's.

## When to use it

Use it when the user asks to compare proposals that already exist, or when one delivery hands over two or more runnable whole-page proposals the user has to choose between, a redesign beside the current page included. Opening it is part of that request, not a new approval step. A single proposal never opens the page.

The other three surfaces keep their jobs:

- Choosing a direction before anything is built stays on the direction round's decision page ([new-work.md](new-work.md)).
- Element-level variants stay in [live.md](live.md) or [generate.md](generate.md).
- The plan and asset review of a comp-led build stays in [component-review.md](component-review.md).

## Prepare the proposals

Keep the content, the primary action, and the target user identical across proposals; only the design changes. A comparison whose proposals differ in copy or scope measures the copy. Each proposal gets one representative screen, not a tour.

Pick the source by what can actually run:

- **A page in the project:** a local address (`url`). Only `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, and `[::1]` are accepted. Add the top-level `serve` (`command`, optional `cwd`) so the page can tell the user how to start the server, and leave the dev server running while the page is open. A server that is down shows that proposal as unavailable; the others still load. A dev server that refuses to be framed renders blank: the page offers "open in a new tab" for every local address, or use a screenshot for that proposal.
- **A standalone mockup:** an HTML file (`source`) in its own self-contained directory such as `.phoenix-ui/compare/variants/<id>/`, with its styles, images, and fonts referenced by relative path. The page serves only that directory. By default the mockup runs no script and makes no request beyond the comparison page's own server, so remote fonts, images, and analytics do not load. Mark a mockup that needs script to render as `"interactive": true`: inline and same-directory classic scripts then run, and it still cannot reach the network. A page that needs a build step, module scripts, `../` paths, or root-relative (`/…`) asset paths goes in as a local address instead.
- **A screenshot:** only when no runnable page can be had. Point `source` at the image and set `pixelRatio` to the device pixel ratio it was captured at (a `phoenix-ui capture --dpr 2` shot is `2`). Screenshots show at their own size and ignore the viewport switch.

When the work changes an existing page, put the current page in too and mark it `"baseline": true`. At most one baseline; the page lists it first and labels it as the current state.

Fill `concept` (one sentence), `typography`, `palette` (color values), and `traits` with what is actually true of each proposal. Leave out what does not apply: an omitted field takes no space on the page. `title` and `question` are optional. The page chrome is Simplified Chinese by default; set `"lang": "en"` in an English session.

Every `source` must sit inside the project directory. The command needs at least two proposals and lists every payload problem in one run.

## Serve and collect

1. `.claude/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui compare --schema` prints the payload shape. Write the payload to a file under `.phoenix-ui/compare/`.
2. `.claude/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui compare --start --payload <file>` validates the payload, starts a detached local server, prints `COMPARE URL:` and `COMPARE KEY:`, and exits. Open that URL for the user: in-app browser first, then the system opener, then showing the URL. `--open` asks the command to use the system opener itself.
3. `.claude/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui compare --wait --key <key>` collects the choice. Repeat it while it exits 3 (`WAITING:`).

On the page the user can view the proposals side by side or one at a time, switch between desktop (1440×900) and mobile (390×844), read a single proposal at 100%, and move between proposals from the keyboard. Small text and fine detail are judged at 100%, not in the scaled-down overview; say so when the choice hangs on them.

**Never submit a choice yourself.** Do not post to the page, script its buttons, or treat your own recommendation as an answer. You may recommend a proposal in chat; the pick is the user's.

| Action | Exit | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--start` | 0 | Serving; URL and key printed |
| `--start` | 1 | Payload rejected (every problem is listed), the key is already serving or still holds an uncollected answer (`--wait` to collect it or `--stop` to discard it), or the server could not listen; a sandboxed command that cannot bind localhost causes the last one, so rerun once through the unsandboxed command channel |
| `--start` | 2 | No browser in this session; nothing was served |
| `--wait` | 0 | `ANSWER:` printed |
| `--wait` | 3 | `WAITING:` no choice yet; run `--wait` again |
| `--wait` | 4 | `PAGE CLOSED:` the page went away without a choice |
| `--wait` | 2 | The server is gone with no answer: a server failure, not a user decision |

## Read the result

`ANSWER:` carries one JSON object: `{"proposalId", "name", "baseline", "liked", "disliked"}`. `proposalId` is the proposal the user picked. `liked` and `disliked` are the user's own words about it; either may be empty. Continue from that proposal, carry what they liked, and change what they disliked.

`BASELINE KEPT:` follows the answer when the pick is the baseline: the user chose to keep the current state. Do not modify the existing implementation, and do not start building one of the other proposals.

## No answer

- **`--wait` exits 4:** ask once more through the structured question tool, naming the same proposals. With still no answer: when the round has a baseline, keep the current state and mark the delivery `choice pending` (`待选` in a Chinese session); when it has none, continue with the proposal you recommend and mark the delivery `not chosen by the user` (`未经用户选择`). Then run `.claude/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui compare --stop --key <key>`.
- **`--start` exits 2:** this session has no browser. Go straight to the structured question tool, under the same fallback rule.
- **`--wait` exits 2:** restart with the same payload through `--start`, reopen the URL for the user, and wait again.

No answer is never a choice. Do not present the recommended proposal as selected, and do not implement a proposal the user has not picked while a baseline exists.

## Report

The delivery states which proposals were compared, which one the user chose and their notes, or that the choice is pending or was not made by the user.
